Overlay patch 0049: shell-requested pause (VR-spec D8 / trap C1).

Every VR exit path — the ornament, the Digital Crown, a system dismissal —
must leave the game PAUSED, because a dismissal is frequently the first half
of a swipe-kill and swipe-kill is SIGKILL: whatever the player was doing when
the headset came off should still be there.

The shell cannot pause MK64 by itself. The only pause entry is the START
handler in race_logic.c, which reads the controller's buttonPressed edge, so
the shell raises a request flag and the handler synthesises exactly that edge
on the next tick of the pausing controller. One tick, one pause, self-clearing
— nothing latches across a mode boundary (trap C2).

Deliberately does NOT force the pause when the game is already paused, and
does not bypass the L/R guard: it is the same code path the player's own START
press takes, which is why it cannot desync the pause state machine.


--- a/src/racing/race_logic.c
+++ b/src/racing/race_logic.c
@@ -785,6 +785,19 @@
                 func_800029B0();
             }
         }
+#ifdef __IOS__
+        /* SOH_IOS (overlay 0049): the shell asks for a pause on every VR exit
+         * (VR-spec D8). Synthesising the START edge here — rather than
+         * writing gIsGamePaused behind the state machine's back — means the
+         * pause takes exactly the path the player's own press takes. */
+        {
+            extern volatile int gSohIosPauseReq;
+            if (gSohIosPauseReq && (gIsGamePaused == 0) && (controller == gControllerOne)) {
+                gSohIosPauseReq = 0;
+                controller->buttonPressed |= START_BUTTON;
+            }
+        }
+#endif
         if ((controller->buttonPressed & START_BUTTON) && (!(controller->button & R_TRIG)) &&
             (!(controller->button & L_TRIG))) {
             func_8028DF00();
